repl_pin — implementation plan
===============================

status: done (2026-07-09)

references I1..I5 (plans/repl_list). pass-through ("pin" => keys
["repl"]). pins are the repl-composition feature: a pinned theory
state becomes an init spec ("pin@NAME") for other repls; staleness
and rebase keep the composition honest. this plan owns the pin
lifecycle tests; unpin/rebase plans reference them.

advertised metadata (the builtin table row)
-------------------------------------------

name          repl_pin
description   "Pin (snapshot) a REPL's current theory state so other
              REPLs can build on it: pass \"pin@NAME\" in
              repl_init's theories to start from the pinned state.
              The REPL must be at theory level, not mid-proof. If the
              pinned REPL is modified afterwards (step, edit,
              truncate), the pin is marked STALE — dependents keep
              working on the old snapshot until you re-pin here and
              repl_rebase there. Re-pinning bumps the pin version."
              (source: mcp_server.py repl_pin :399, plus the version/
              stale mechanics of ir.ML:510.)
inputSchema   {"type": "object",
               "properties": {"repl": {"type": "string"}},
               "required": ["repl"]}
annotations   {"readOnlyHint": false, "idempotentHint": false,
               "openWorldHint": false}
              (not readOnly: it mutates the repl's pin field; not
              idempotent: each call bumps the version.)
outputSchema  none.

name/argument mapping across the layers
---------------------------------------

  mcp client edge   tools/call "repl_pin", {"repl": "A"}
  scala builtin row backend.ir("pin", [(repl, A)])
  ml dispatcher     "pin" -> Ir.pin (get "repl")
  engine            ir.ML:510 — refuses mid-proof; version = old+1;
                    out "Pinned \"A\""

implementation steps
--------------------

1. [this tool] builtin row + handler; errors -> isError.
2. tests; rebuild per rules.

assumptions and how to test them
--------------------------------

T1  mid-proof pin is refused with the engine's message.
    test [ml unit]: step "lemma True", pin -> error "in a proof
    state"; finish the proof, pin -> ok.
T2  the pin round trip: pin A, init B ["pin@A"] -> B's base state has
    A's definitions; B's origin prints "pin@A".
    test [ml unit]: A defines a constant, pin, init B from pin@A,
    step B using the constant -> ok.
T3  staleness: mutating A after the pin marks it stale (repls/show
    say "pinned [stale]"), and a NEW init from pin@A errors "Pin ...
    is stale" — existing dependents are untouched.
    test [ml unit]: pin A, init B, step A -> show A stale; init C
    ["pin@A"] -> error; B still steps; re-pin A -> init C ok.
    NOTE while implementing: verify which mutations mark stale (step
    goes through claim/release — check mark_pin_stale call sites);
    pin the actual trigger set in this test and the description.
T4  version bump feeds rebase: after re-pin, dependent B's specs
    resolve to the new version (asserted via plans/repl_rebase T1;
    here just: pin twice -> ok both times).
T5  full chain.
    test [e2e]: build-a-base workflow — repl A with definitions, pin,
    repl B from pin@A proves a lemma using them.

